Here’s a comprehensive yet interview-friendly guide to Low-Level Design (LLD) basics. I’ll cover the core concepts, approach, common patterns, and tips, so you can explain it clearly in an interview.

1. What is Low-Level Design (LLD)?

LLD focuses on designing individual components, classes, and methods of a system.

It’s about how the system will be implemented at the code level, unlike HLD, which focuses on architecture and modules.

Goal: Ensure scalability, maintainability, reusability, and readability of code.

2. Key Concepts
a) Classes & Objects

Identify entities in the problem domain.

Define attributes (fields) and behavior (methods).

Example:
For a Library Management System:

class Book {
    String title;
    String author;
    boolean isAvailable;

    void borrow() { ... }
    void returnBook() { ... }
}

b) Relationships

Association: One class uses another.

Library has many Books.

Aggregation: "Has-a" relationship where objects can exist independently.

Library has a list of Books.

Composition: Strong "Has-a" relationship; lifetime tied to owner.

Order has OrderItems; deleting Order deletes items.

Inheritance: "Is-a" relationship; promotes code reuse.

AdminUser extends User.

Interfaces: Define contracts that classes must follow.

c) Methods & Responsibilities

Follow Single Responsibility Principle (SRP): each class should have a single responsibility.

Use encapsulation to hide internal data and provide access via getters/setters.

d) Design Patterns (Common in LLD)

Creational: Singleton, Factory, Builder

Structural: Adapter, Decorator, Composite

Behavioral: Observer, Strategy, Command

Example (Observer pattern in Notification Service):

interface Observer {
    void update(String message);
}

class User implements Observer {
    public void update(String message) { ... }
}

e) Error Handling

Define exceptions and how classes handle errors.

Example: BookNotAvailableException in a Library system.

f) Data Structures & Storage

Decide which data structures to use for optimal performance: Lists, Maps, Sets, Queues, etc.

Consider thread safety if your system is concurrent.

3. LLD Steps in an Interview

Understand the problem clearly

Ask about constraints, scale, edge cases.

Identify entities and their relationships

Draw classes and their associations.

Define methods and responsibilities

What each class should do, inputs/outputs, exceptions.

Add design patterns if needed

Justify why a pattern fits a particular scenario.

Consider edge cases and scalability

How will your design handle millions of users or requests?

Think about modularity and maintainability

Ensure classes are loosely coupled and highly cohesive.

4. Example: Simple LLD

Problem: Design a Ticket Booking System

Entities:

User (name, email)

Event (name, date, seats)

Ticket (id, user, event)

BookingService (bookTicket(), cancelTicket())

Relationships:

User can have multiple Tickets → aggregation

BookingService manages tickets → association

5. Key Interview Tips

Always draw UML diagrams: classes, relationships, methods.

Use interfaces and abstract classes where applicable.

Explain why you chose a pattern or data structure.

Consider scalability, concurrency, and error handling.

Keep the design simple, modular, and extensible.

